course: biology
lesson: 69
title: Senses: eyes and ears
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your eyes catch light and your ears catch vibrations in the air, and both turn what they catch into nerve signals that the brain makes into pictures and sounds.
explain: Walk out of a dark cinema into bright sunshine and you squint: the black dot in your eye, the pupil, shrinks fast, because the coloured ring around it, the iris, is a muscle that lets in less light.
explain: Inside the eye a lens, like a magnifying glass, throws an upside-down picture onto the back of the eye, and the brain turns it the right way up and even fills in a small blind spot where you see nothing at all.
explain: In your ear a tiny drum, three of the smallest bones in your body and a snail-shaped tube full of hair cells turn a beating drum at the school concert into signals the brain hears as music.
explain: The ear also keeps your balance, which is why the world spins after a roundabout; and many favourite «facts», like carrots giving night vision or bats being blind, are myths.
fact: The pupil is a hole that lets light into the eye; the coloured iris around it is a muscle that makes the pupil smaller in bright light and bigger in the dark.
fact: The lens in the eye works like a magnifying glass and makes an upside-down picture on the retina at the back of the eye, and the brain turns it the right way up.
fact: Where the optic nerve leaves the eye there are no light cells, so each eye has a small blind spot that the brain usually fills in so we do not notice it.
fact: Cone cells for colour come in three kinds, most sensitive to red, green and blue light, and the brain mixes their signals into all colours; rod cells see in dim light but not in colour.
fact: About 1 in 12 boys and men and about 1 in 200 girls and women are colour blind, most often mixing up reds and greens.
fact: Mantis shrimps have 12 to 16 kinds of colour cells against our 3 and can see ultraviolet light, but tests suggest they are not better than us at telling close colours apart.
fact: Carrots have vitamin A, which the eye needs to see in dim light, but eating extra carrots does not give night vision; the story spread in the Second World War and, many historians say, helped hide the use of radar.
fact: Bats are not blind: all bats can see, and many also find insects in the dark by echolocation, making high squeaks and listening for the echoes.
fact: Sound is vibration in the air: it shakes the eardrum, three tiny bones called the hammer, anvil and stirrup pass it on, and hair cells in the snail-shaped cochlea turn it into nerve signals.
fact: Sound reaches the nearer ear a tiny fraction of a second sooner and a little louder, and the brain uses these differences to tell where a sound comes from.
fact: The inner ear has three loops filled with fluid; when you spin, the fluid swirls, and after you stop it keeps moving for a moment, so you feel dizzy.
fact: Very loud sounds can damage the hair cells in the cochlea, which in people do not grow back; ringing ears after fireworks are a warning sign, and ear defenders help.
fact: An echo is sound bouncing off a hard surface and coming back; sound travels about 340 metres a second in air, so an echo from a cliff 170 metres away comes back after about one second.
exercise: Why do your pupils get smaller in bright sunshine? | The iris muscle shrinks the pupil to let in less light | The strong sun burns the edge of the pupil and makes it smaller | The eye is slowly closing so you can sleep | Tears fill up the pupil
exercise: What does the lens in your eye do? | Makes an upside-down picture on the back of the eye | Gives your eyes their blue, green or brown colour | Washes dust out of the eye every time you blink | Lets you see clearly even in total darkness
exercise: Who turns the upside-down picture in your eye the right way up? | Your brain | Your eye's lens | Your eyelids | Your salty tears
exercise: Why does each eye have a blind spot? | There are no light cells where the optic nerve leaves | Dust always settles in that one spot and blocks the light there | The eyelashes cover that part of the eye | The lens is cracked in that spot
exercise: How do you see all the colours in a box of crayons? | Three kinds of cone cells send signals the brain mixes | Each colour in the box has its own special cell in the eye | The crayons glow with a light of their own | The lens paints the colours on the eye
exercise: Which cells let you see in dim light? | Rod cells | Cone cells | Red blood cells | Hair cells
exercise: About how many boys and men are colour blind? | About 1 in 12 | About 1 in every 2 | About 1 in 1,000 | None of them at all
exercise: What is special about mantis shrimp eyes? | They have 12 to 16 kinds of colour cells and see ultraviolet | They are completely blind and find their food by smell instead | They have only one kind of colour cell | They can see straight through rocks
exercise: Do carrots let you see in the dark? | No, they help keep eyes healthy but give no night vision | Yes, eating lots of them every day gives you real night vision | Yes, but only the bright orange ones | Only if you eat them raw at night
exercise: Are bats blind? | No, all bats can see, and many also use echolocation | Yes, every bat is completely blind from the day it is born | Yes, but they see with their ears | Only the bats that live in caves
exercise: What happens first when a drum's sound reaches your ear? | The vibrations shake the eardrum | The cochlea turns the sound into light | The tiny bones in the ear start to grow | The ear canal fills up with water
exercise: Which part of the ear turns vibrations into nerve signals? | Hair cells in the snail-shaped cochlea | The outer flap of the ear that sticks out | The sticky yellow earwax deep in the ear | The thin eardrum stretched across the tube
exercise: How can you tell where a dog is barking with your eyes shut? | The sound reaches one ear a tiny bit sooner and louder | You can smell which way the dog is from the air | One ear hears dogs and the other ear hears only people | The bark makes your nose point at it
exercise: Why do you feel dizzy after spinning on a roundabout? | Fluid in the inner ear keeps swirling after you stop | Your brain gets far too little blood while you spin round | Your eyes get tired of all the colours | Your legs forget how to stand up
exercise: Why do your ears ring after a loud firework display? | Hair cells in the cochlea have been strained by loud sound | Your eardrum has filled up with smoke from all the rockets | The fireworks left a sound stuck in your ear | Your brain is still counting the bangs
exercise: What is an echo? | Sound bouncing off a hard surface and coming back | A second voice that the cave makes up all by itself | Sound that turns into wind | A sound that is stuck in your ear
test: bruv eat ur carrots, you'll see in the dark like a cat. true? | No, carrots keep eyes healthy but don't give night vision | Yes, ten carrots a day and you see in the dark like a cat | Yes, the orange colour glows in your eyes | No, carrots make you blind at night
test: In a cartoon a bat bumps into everything because «bats are blind». Put it right. | Bats can see, and many dodge things in the dark by echolocation | Bats really are blind, so in real life they crash all the time | Bats see only with their ears | Bats only ever fly in daylight
test: What would happen if your iris could not change the pupil's size? | Bright light would dazzle you and dim light would be harder to see in | You would only see in black and white, like an old film on television | You could not see at all | Nothing, the pupil does not matter
test: Ada covers her left eye and moves a paper with a dot and a cross. The cross vanishes. Why? | Its picture has landed on her blind spot | Her eye has suddenly stopped working for good | The cross was rubbed out | Her brain is tired of crosses
test: My mate says his eyes see everything upside down but his brain flips it. Nonsense? | No, it is right: the lens makes an upside-down picture and the brain turns it | Yes, it is nonsense: eyes always see everything the right way up from the start | Yes, only cameras flip pictures | Yes, babies see upside down for ever
test: My uncle can't tell the red and green apples apart. What is most likely? | He is colour blind, which is common in men | He needs to eat a lot more carrots every day | His blind spot has grown big enough to hide colours | He needs glasses to tell colours apart properly
test: shout HELLO in a cave and it comes back a second later. how far is the wall then? | About 170 metres, as sound goes there and back | About 340 metres, because sound only goes one way | About 17 metres | About 1 kilometre
test: Why should you wear ear defenders near very loud fireworks or engines? | Loud sounds can damage hair cells in the ear that do not grow back | Ear defenders make you hear better | Loud sounds can make your ears fall off | Fireworks blow lots of earwax and smoke deep into your ears and block them
test: An owl hunts mice in the dark by sound alone. Why are its two ears useful? | Each ear hears the sound a little differently, giving the direction | Two ears let the owl hear the colours of the mice in the dark | One ear is for day and one for night | Two ears keep its head balanced
test: In a game your hero spins round twenty times and walks in a straight line at once. What really happens? | He would wobble, as fluid in his inner ear keeps swirling | He would walk even faster than before because he is warmed up | He would fly like a top | Nothing at all happens
test: A mantis shrimp has 16 kinds of colour cells and we have 3. Does it see colours much better than us? | Not exactly: tests suggest it is not better at telling close colours apart | Yes, it sees five times better | Yes, it can read in the dark | No, it sees no colours at all
exam: Identify the function of the iris in the regulation of vision. | It adjusts the diameter of the pupil according to the light | It forms the inverted image upon the retina at the rear of the eye | It perceives colour | It produces tears
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the blind spot of the human eye? | It is the region devoid of light cells where the optic nerve departs | It is a defect present only in the elderly | It is a region of damage caused by prolonged exposure to bright sunlight | It is located at the front surface of the eye
exam: It is asserted that the consumption of carrots confers vision in darkness. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false, although vitamin A is needed for vision in dim light | The assertion is true without qualification | The assertion is true for children only | The assertion is true of carrots cooked and consumed shortly before nightfall
exam: Ascertain the cause of the sensation of dizziness experienced upon ceasing to spin. | Fluid within the inner ear continues to move | The eardrum is displaced | Blood ceases to reach the eyes | The cochlea is temporarily filled with air
